{"availability":"available","full":"fegyi001/osmgwc","name":"osmgwc","description":"A step-by-step tutorial to create a geowebcache layer from OSM data which will look a lot like Google Maps","category":"Other","language":"CSS","stars":85,"forks":41,"created":"2016-11-22T18:37:54Z","updated":"2022-07-30T18:54:10Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":[],"demo":"http://188.166.116.137:8080/geoserver/gwc/demo/osmWsp:osm_hungary?gridSet=EPSG:900913&format=image/png","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.709Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.709Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.709Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.709Z","stars":85,"forks":41}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A step-by-step tutorial to create a geowebcache layer from OSM data which will look a lot like Google Maps","Osmgwc is a step-by-step tutorial of how to create a fast cached WMS layer which will hopefully look similar to Google Maps' design. The main difference is that it will be based on the free and open source OpenStreetMap (OSM) data, stored in a PostGIS database and served with GeoServer. Another difference is that it will be stored in the spatial reference system (SRS) of your choice.","If you follow the steps below, you will download OSM data, create an empty database, populate it with geodata in the desired SRS, then split the data into different tables (settlements, rivers etc.), add them as layers in GeoServer, style them with CSS styles, create a combined layer group, define a grid for your SRS, then finally publish a cached layer."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/fegyi001/osmgwc/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"fa387de68adfb1243848b3e0d4fd32c05869b42edea2ff62a634fafbd63fe127","extractedAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.812Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T16:06:16.812Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"A step-by-step tutorial to create a geowe